Why Package Density and Yield Are Moving Together
Mobile devices and wearables reward compact interconnects, while high-performance computing requires bandwidth, heat management and dependable assembly. Fan-out processes can shorten electrical paths and accommodate dense routing, but thin molded or reconstituted wafers are vulnerable to bow and process variation.

Manufacturers evaluate total package economics rather than a single substrate price. Photolithography steps, mold compound behavior, redistribution layers and bump reliability can change usable yield. A process that appears efficient on a pilot run may need additional metrology, control plans and inspection before serving a long-life automotive program.

